n outscored 242 to 221. The offense has been carrying the team, ranking 13th in EPA per play while the defense sits 25th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive trait is the lowest no-huddle rate in the league.","offense":"The Patriots offense has been a little above average by EPA per play so far. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Schematically, they stand out for the lowest no-huddle rate in the league. Drake Maye has run the passing game, with DeMario Douglas leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Patriots have one of the worst defenses in the league this season, ranking 25th or worse in four of the five main efficiency measures. Their one bright spot has been the pass rush, which ranks 6th in sack rate. Elijah Ponder leads the team with 7.5 sacks, and Christian Barmore has added 4.5 despite playing only 46% of the snaps. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is an eight man box rate that ranks 5th highest in the league.","special":"The Patriots have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 79% of field goals, which is well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 19% of the money on the books. Their quarterbacks were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Drake Maye has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 2,330 yards. Targets have been spread around, with DeMario Douglas and Hunter Henry leading a group where nobody has more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield has been a committee led by Rhamondre Stevenson and Corey Kiner. On defense, Elijah Ponder leads the team with 7.5 sacks and Kevin Byard has picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Josh McDaniels is calling the offense and Zak Kuhr is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2022 Saints under Pete Carmichael and the 2025 Texans under Nick Caley. Their defense looks most like the 2024 Steelers and the 2024 Jets under Jeff Ulbrich.","trends":"The offense went from 1st in EPA per play in 2025 to 13th so far this season, a big step back. The defense moved from 11th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 25th so far this season, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was number of defenders in the box, which went from 6.2 to 6.47.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}